  Those are why the vendoring detail below carries a tail of archives holding
  exactly one of a module's classes. Each such class is named with its module in
  that detail, and none is ever used as a witness by either measurement.

  'original-' pre-shade sibling pairs in the set        : 37
    - class-identical to their shaded main artifact     : 11
    - still holding genuinely pre-shade content         : 26

  maven-jar-plugin skips a main JAR it considers up to date, so a later shade
  execution in an unclean build directory backs up an ALREADY shaded artifact
  under the 'original-' name. Where that happened the pre-shade sibling holds the
  shaded class set, measured above rather than assumed. It changes nothing about a
  module's own exclusivity - a class shared with a same-module sibling still
  qualifies - but such a sibling does enter every OTHER module's subtraction, so
  the fact is published with the tables it bears on. No archive was dropped on
  account of it: AAP 0.3.2 forbids trimming the input set, and dropping a shaded
  archive to manufacture uniqueness is exactly what the witness rule forbids.

