  WHAT THAT MEANS, STATED PLAINLY.

  Even a fully compliant graph over the complete 191-archive input set could
  only be verdicted for the 30 modules that HAVE a witness. The remaining
  8 would still be NO VERDICT OBTAINABLE, because their shortfall is a property
  of the witness rule against Spark's shaded artifacts - a fat artifact vendors
  every class and the Maven descriptor of the smaller modules it repackages, so
  nothing in the smaller module's primary artifact is exclusive to it - and NOT a
  property of any graph, of any heap, or of the frontend. It is not caused by the
  graph in use being the provisioned 62-archive one, and it would not be removed
  by building a graph over all of them.

  This is reported, not repaired. AAP 0.5.1 admits exactly two witness kinds and
  no third, and AAP 0.9.4 requires a value that could not be established to be
  named as such rather than omitted or softened. So no third witness kind is
  invented here, presence is never substituted for exclusivity, no shared package
  prefix is used as evidence, and no shaded archive is dropped from either input
  set to manufacture uniqueness. Removing the shortfall needs a change to the
  witness rule itself - an injective archive-provenance witness such as the staged
  coordinate and digest retained in graph metadata - which is a human/AAP decision
  outside this pass.

  F4's condition REPRODUCES first-hand in this clone: 8 of 38 JAR-producing modules have
